fof_version: '1.0'
type: enemy
id: latchman
name: Latchman
ruleset:
  modules:
  - id: base
    version: 1.0.0
enemy:
  # v0.3: Named, durability 4, attack +2, heavy armor, rating 11.
  level: 3
  role: elite
  armor: 2
  morale: 12
  weapon: Iron hands
  wants: The standing order carried out, to the letter.
  special: 'THE STANDING ORDER — the first time it is engaged it states its instruction aloud,
    and the instruction is always the true one and always decades old. It acts on nothing
    outside the order: a party that satisfies the letter of it walks past while the latchman
    holds the door.'
  when_bloodied: It stops following anyone and stands in the doorway, Defending (no attack;
    attacks on it are Hard), repeating the order to anyone who will listen.
  tells: It has not moved since you came in. When it speaks, the voice is like a drawer closing.
  breaks: Nothing to break. It stops when the order is satisfied, countermanded by the authority
    that gave it, or made impossible.
  twists:
  - The order is "not before the eleventh bell", and no bell here has rung eleven in sixty
    years.
  - The order names a guild livery. One still exists, in a chest, in a family that will want
    something for it.
  - The order covers the door. Nothing in it mentions the wall.
  - Latchlings keep the lesser doors, and each will recite a different piece of the building's
    rules.
  - The order lets the owner's heir through, and one of the party might count.
  - The building has been divided up, and half the order's words now describe the shop next
    door.
  nastier: It holds two standing orders from two authorities, they contradict each other,
    and it enforces both.
  description: >-
    Man-height, iron-jointed, with a face that is a plate with two holes in it and a voice
    like a drawer closing. It is standing exactly where it was told to stand. It has been
    standing there since before the building's owners stopped existing.
  notes: >-
    Level 3 elite, armor 2: no overrides. Elite because it sat at the top of the old middle
    rank with the veteran soldier and is the harder of the two to wear down; fearless because
    it has nothing to break.


    Literal. Absolutely, ruinously literal, and entirely without malice about it. Its only
    target is whoever crossed the line its instruction describes. Players who listen to the
    order are being handed the solution.


    Appears: one per building that mattered, sometimes with three or four latchlings on the
    lesser doors.
  reskin_of: null
